  :cell/finding "Ed25519 publisher attestation binds marker, sealed provenance, artifact identity and target, and its own docstring says it does not replace output admission. Dual-compiler agreement and independent rebuild are absent, so a compromised compiler is attested rather than detected."}

 {:db/id -11 :cell/plane "key" :cell/stride "information-disclosure"
  :cell/linddun "data-disclosure" :cell/status :measured-residue :source/dataset "privacy-plane"
  :cell/evidence "orgs/kotoba-lang/kotobase scripts/measure-disclosure-grant.cljs, pin faa5cc00419c"
  :cell/finding "Measured 2026-09-10 by RUNNING it: 94 probes over release! and delivery-verification, each asserting the reason literal; 94 matched prediction, 7 released, 87 refused across 19 distinct refusal literals. The expiry boundary sits where the source says (now == expires-at refuses :request-expired, one unit earlier releases, now == not-before releases) and mutating before? from < to <= flips exactly those probes. A wrong recipient is refused for the reason it names: dropping the single recipient equality releases exactly 2 probes and no others. No port shape is permission -- 36 probes of nil, integer, map, keyword, non-callable and outright dissoc release 0, and a port answering anything not literally true releases 0 of 15. A refusal before release writes nothing."
  :cell/residue "delivery-verification hardwires (constantly true) as its nonce ledger, so the recipient side verifies the same delivery twice with no distinction; its one non-test consumer, ayatori.disclosure/decryptor, re-reads context and refuses :authority-changed on an epoch shift but adds no ledger. Separately, :authority-epoch-revoked is unreachable from this entry -- every epoch disagreement is caught earlier by equality as :scope-mismatch or :request-mismatch, and removing those two comparisons is what makes the literal appear, on 5 probes."
  :cell/not-measured "no real cryptography (verify!/sign! are string-equality fixtures); HPKE AAD binding; the JVM path; whether any caller wraps delivery-verification with its own nonce ledger (the one that exists does not)"}]
